sequenceDiagram
  %% Target design, container runtime (ADR 0250 step 3, docs/reference/development-containers.md#the-default-instance): the automatic development deployment of docs/reference/deployments.md, in the default Instance's container. The Gateway checks the branch head before it starts development policy; Swamp keeps the retry and admission state. When the charts move into place, this becomes the development branch of the deploy chart.
  %% status: draft
  %% command: none · automatic deployment policy
  %% kind: instance.deploy
  autonumber
  %% lane GW icon=gateway pill=intent tone=blue
  %% lane SW icon=workflow pill=executes tone=green
  %% lane N icon=server pill=target tone=amber
  %% lane C icon=server pill=target tone=purple
  participant GW as Gateway<br/>Laravel API
  participant SW as Swamp<br/>policy · execution
  participant N as app-dev Node<br/>reached over ssh
  participant C as Container<br/>default Instance
  loop every minute
    Note over GW: Check eligible defaults<br/>recorded placement and seed
    %%> What it does:
    %%> - selects only active container defaults with a seed snapshot, auto_deploy on and active app-dev placement, using the same ready-default rule as conn:project:list
    %%> - the flag defaults on when a new container default becomes ready; enable and disable change that one flag for one Instance
    %%> - a data migration enables existing ready defaults once; checkout Instances and production settings remain unchanged
    %%> - never selects checkout Instances: they are the user's working trees, not managed seed containers
    %%> - runs the unique scan on the existing tasks queue, not the single-worker operations queue; an execution lock prevents overlap even after delayed queue delivery
    %%> - reads each eligible Project's default branch once through the cached source reader, with the existing 60-second Git read limit; no environment-template refresh
    alt seed matches branch head
      Note over GW: Nothing to deploy<br/>no Swamp run
    else default is behind
      GW->>SW: Run development policy<br/>only the selected Instance IDs
      %%> What it does:
      %%> - shares the retained behavior identity and development state; keeps pending admissions, backoff and retry limits
      %%> - reads a retained pending Operation by ID; ended Operations settle for their admitted commit; success reports up_to_date only when the current runtime seed matches
      %%> - failed, cancelled, interrupted and timed-out Operations share one per-commit retry accounting with history: ten minutes, twenty minutes, then stop after three failures; null-commit history retains the admission pin
      %%> - a lost admission response follows the development receipt by key; an absent admission is fenced before abandoning the intent and applying the same retry accounting
      %%> - an old terminal checkpoint or failure cannot block a newer green head after an Instance moves into a container
      %%> - never discovers or admits production work; its paused production schedule stays unchanged
      Note over SW: Check the moved head<br/>Project merge_check
      %%> What it does:
      %%> - reads the head commit's configured check runs through the same Gateway source contract and verdict rule as production
      %%> - the named GitHub Actions runs must all complete successfully on exactly that SHA; running or missing checks wait, failed checks do not deploy
      %%> - development checks only the current head and reads it again on the next tick; it never chooses an older green commit
      %%> - with no Project merge_check, permits the moved head without a check, as production does
  SW->>GW: Admit the development deployment<br/>instance:deploy:automatic:development
  %%> What it does:
  %%> - Swamp rechecks development-specific eligibility from the Gateway, not the eligibility shared with production
  %%> - the Gateway refuses non-development Instances and rechecks the ready container-default rule under the Instance lock
  %%> - the Gateway admits `instance.deploy` for that exact commit, with the Instance lock and a repository read token in the vault
  GW->>SW: Start run, full intent<br/>instance-deploy-direct
  %%> Before execution:
  %%> - automatic development work rechecks the same eligibility policy while building its input, including work queued before a release
  %%> - a checkout default refuses automatic execution; its explicit manual deployment remains allowed
  opt the default Instance is asleep
    SW->>N: Start the container<br/>incus start
  end
  SW->>N: Fetch into the mirror<br/>Ansible · tags fetch
  %%> What it does:
  %%> - fetches into the Node's mirror and pushes the branch into the container as a bundle; the token never enters the container
  %%> - a pinned commit off the branch's first-parent chain refuses `deployment.commit_not_on_branch`
  SW->>C: Check out in the container<br/>Ansible · tags checkout
  %%> What it does:
  %%> - the same checks as a checkout default: a dirty tree refuses `deployment.checkout_dirty`, a diverged branch `deployment.branch_diverged`
  SW->>C: Run dev deploy steps<br/>incus exec · vault copy
  %%> What it does:
  %%> - the Project's development deploy steps, as `orbit`: they install changed dependencies and migrate
  SW->>C: Install the Processes<br/>Ansible · tags processes
  %%> What it does:
  %%> - installs the Project's new and changed development Process definitions as `orbit-*` units, with their environment files from the vault
  %%> - restarts a changed unit and removes a former environment file
  SW->>N: Take the seed snapshot<br/>Ansible · tags seed
  %%> What it does:
  %%> - stops the default's Processes for a moment, snapshots the container and starts them again, so clones copy no half-written file
  %%> - deletes older seed snapshots that no clone uses
  %%> - starting the units again restarts every running Process on the new commit, such as a queue worker
  SW->>C: Check the container<br/>Ansible · tags check
  %%> What it does:
  %%> - the branch is checked out at the commit and the units are active, else `deployment.branch_changed` or `deployment.processes_inactive`
  alt every required step passes
    SW-->>GW: Run succeeded<br/>terminal event
    Note over GW: Record the seed commit
    %%> Result:
    %%> - new clones start at this snapshot; existing clones do not change
  else a required step fails
    SW--xGW: Run failed<br/>failed step
    %%> Result:
    %%> - no new seed snapshot is taken, so clones keep starting at the last commit that deployed; Swamp's policy retries after its backoff
  end
  end
  end
